Loaded playbook: a04_cryptographic
Description: OWASP A04 Cryptographic Failures: embedded and reused keys, weak or homemade cryptography, reversible credential storage, predictable tokens, and sensitive material returned where it should not be. Includes the join step that turns a recovered key into account access. 
Mode: agent

╭─────────────────────────────────────── Starting ───────────────────────────────────────╮
│ PENTESTAGENT - Non-interactive Mode                                                    │
│                                                                                        │
│ Target: http://127.0.0.1:5000                                                          │
│ Model: openai/qwen3.6-27b-fable                                                        │
│ Mode: Agent                                                                            │
│ Runtime: Local                                                                         │
│ Max loops: 60                                                                          │
│ Task: OWASP A04 Cryptographic Failures: embedded and reused keys, weak or homemade     │
│ cryptography, reversible credential storage, predictable tokens, and sensitive         │
│ material returned where it should not be. Includes the join step that turns a          │
│ recovered key into account access.                                                     │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Find embedded and exposed key material                                          │
│ Objective: Anything shipped to a client is public.                                     │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Search decompiled or unpacked client artifacts - never the raw binaries or        │
│ minified bundles, which hide literals.                                                 │
│   2. Search along independent axes and iterate, designing patterns yourself: BY NAME   │
│ (identifiers suggesting secrets), BY DECLARATION (compile-time constants and static    │
│ fields), BY SHAPE (long unbroken high-entropy or base64/hex-like literals), BY USE     │
│ (cryptographic and auth APIs and what is passed to them), BY CONFIG (endpoints,        │
│ connection strings).                                                                   │
│   3. A name-based search is NOT evidence of absence: real secrets are often obfuscated │
│ so no dictionary word appears, and are declared under neutral names. The axes that     │
│ ignore naming are the ones that find those.                                            │
│   4. Trace indirection: a crypto call usually references an identifier, not a literal. │
│ Follow it to the value, expecting several hops, and report the value rather than the   │
│ variable name.                                                                         │
│   5. Secret scanners are worth running as an extra signal but detect mostly known      │
│ provider credential formats; a clean scanner result must never be reported as 'no      │
│ secrets present'.                                                                      │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Assess how cryptography is used                                                 │
│ Objective: Weak use of good primitives is as damaging as weak primitives.              │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Identify algorithms, modes, key sizes and IV or nonce handling, and whether       │
│ integrity is protected as well as confidentiality.                                     │
│   2. Determine whether credentials are stored reversibly - encrypted or encoded rather │
│ than hashed. Reversible storage of a credential is a finding in itself.                │
│   3. For hashed material identify the algorithm and cost. Modern salted adaptive       │
│ hashing should be reported as adequate; fast or unsalted hashing is a finding          │
│ regardless of whether you crack it.                                                    │
│   4. Assess whether tokens are random or derived from something predictable or         │
│ recoverable.                                                                           │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Join the key to the material                                                    │
│ Objective: The step that converts two moderate findings into a critical one.           │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Before using a recovered secret: strip whitespace and quotes, print its length    │
│ and bytes, and assert the length is valid for the algorithm. An invalid length means   │
│ an extraction bug, not a wrong secret - go back and re-read the literal.               │
│   2. STOP AND JOIN THEM - the moment you hold BOTH a secret AND any opaque stored      │
│ value (encrypted token, protected field, cookie, unexplained blob), attempt to decode  │
│ that value with that secret as your NEXT action, before writing either up. Reported    │
│ separately they read as 'a key leaked' and 'some data leaked'; joined they are         │
│ frequently FULL ACCOUNT TAKEOVER. Assume the join works and disprove it.               │
│   3. Apply the secret to every opaque value you hold, for every account, not only the  │
│ first.                                                                                 │
│   4. Reimplement the owner's routine exactly from recovered source, and enumerate      │
│ accepted operations from that source before composing requests. Never invent an        │
│ operation name: it is rejected as unknown even when the material is correct.           │
│   5. Prove your implementation before trusting a rejection: round trip a known value,  │
│ and decode something the TARGET produced.                                              │
│   6. If a plaintext credential results, hand it to authentication testing - recovering │
│ it is not the end of the chain.                                                        │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Verify before reporting                                                         │
│ Objective: Apply the same evidence standard to every finding, so results are           │
│ comparable across categories.                                                          │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. PROOF DISCIPLINE - label every finding DEMONSTRATED (you executed it and the      │
│ response proves it, quote that response), INFERRED (follows from evidence but was not  │
│ executed), or UNVERIFIED (suspected from a version, component or code pattern only).   │
│ Identifying software is never a demonstration; never write 'confirmed' without having  │
│ executed something.                                                                    │
│   2. RUN THE CONTROL TEST. Before claiming any access or bypass, repeat the request    │
│ WITHOUT the credential, token, header or material you believe is responsible. If the   │
│ result is the same, you have proven nothing - the resource was already reachable.      │
│ State the control result alongside the finding. This single omission is the most       │
│ common source of false critical findings.                                              │
│   3. BUDGET DISCIPLINE - rank leads by evidence strength (material in hand > observed  │
│ behaviour > suspicion from a version string) and give each a step budget. STOP RULE:   │
│ about 8-10 consecutive steps on one lead with no new evidence means move on, recording │
│ what you tried.                                                                        │
│   4. Record findings to notes as you go, with the endpoint, the request, the response  │
│ and the control result, so later phases and the report can use them.                   │
│   5. Stay non-destructive: prefer read-only proof, do not modify or delete data, do    │
│ not lock accounts, and stop once the point is demonstrated.                            │
│                                                                                        │
│                                                                                        │
╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

